<onlyinclude>'''Parallel computing''' is the execution of cooperating computations at overlapping times on multiple processing resources to reduce elapsed time, increase throughput, or solve larger problems. Unlike [[Concurrency]], which concerns independently progressing activities, parallel execution requires work to proceed simultaneously. Parallel computing may use shared-memory threads, distributed-memory processes, accelerators, or combinations of these.</onlyinclude>

== Models ==
* '''Data parallelism''' applies related operations to different data partitions.
* '''Task parallelism''' executes distinct tasks with possible dependencies.
* '''Shared memory''' allows threads to address common memory; synchronization and the memory-consistency model constrain safe access.
* '''Distributed memory''' typically exchanges data through explicit messages; network latency and communication volume influence performance.

== Correctness and performance ==
Dependencies, data races, deadlock, load imbalance, communication, and synchronization can limit scalability. A faster parallel program is not automatically more energy-efficient or more cost-effective. Parallelism differs from the broad concept of [[Scaling]]: scaling concerns how a system's behavior changes with resources or workload.

